<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Move from one measurement to several</h2>
<p>A one-dimensional normal distribution describes one number. A <strong>multivariate normal distribution</strong> describes several related numbers at once. For example, one generative-AI request might be described by both its output length and its response time. Those numbers are stored together in a <strong>vector</strong>, which is simply an ordered list of values.</p>
<p>A <strong>covariance matrix</strong> records both the spread of each measurement and how pairs of measurements change together. Its diagonal entries are variances. An off-diagonal entry is a covariance: a positive value means the two measurements tend to rise together, a negative value means one tends to fall as the other rises, and a value near zero means there is little linear relationship. In two dimensions, this structure can be drawn as an ellipse whose width, height, and direction summarize the matrix.</p>
<p>Equation (7.1) defines covariance. The bold &mu; is the mean vector; read &mu; as <strong>mu</strong> (&ldquo;mew&rdquo;). The bold &Sigma; is capital <strong>sigma</strong> (&ldquo;SIG-ma&rdquo;). Here &Sigma; names a matrix&mdash;it is different from the &sum; summation operator. The symbol 𝔼 means <strong>expected value</strong>, which you can read here as the long-run average over many observations. Superscript <em>T</em> means <strong>transpose</strong>: it turns a column into a row so the multiplication produces a matrix.</p>
<p><strong>What you will do:</strong> read covariance matrices, connect them to plotted ellipses, and generate correlated data by transforming simple standard-normal samples.</p>
<p><strong>Video focus (under 10 minutes):</strong> interpret a two-dimensional covariance matrix and generate an elliptical Gaussian by scaling, rotating, and translating standard-normal samples.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 7.1. The covariance matrix capital Sigma equals the expected value of vector X minus mean vector mu, multiplied by X minus mu transpose."><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">&Sigma;</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">X</mi><mo>&minus;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">X</mi><mo>&minus;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(7.1)</span></div>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Elliptical geometry</h2>
<p>Different diagonal variances stretch the ellipse by different amounts along the coordinate axes. Nonzero covariance changes its principal directions, which usually appears as a rotated ellipse. Equation (7.2) is the full density. You do not need to memorize it. Read its pieces instead: <em>d</em> is the number of dimensions; lowercase &pi; is <strong>pi</strong> (&ldquo;pie&rdquo;); |&Sigma;| is the <strong>determinant</strong>, a single number describing overall spread; &Sigma;<sup>&minus;1</sup> is the <strong>inverse covariance</strong>, which adjusts distance for the ellipse&rsquo;s scale and direction; and <strong>exp</strong> makes points farther from &mu; receive lower density.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 7.2. The multivariate normal density p of vector x given mean vector mu and covariance matrix Sigma equals one over two pi to the d over two times determinant Sigma to the one half, multiplied by exp of negative one half times x minus mu transpose, Sigma inverse, x minus mu."><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&Sigma;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&pi;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&Sigma;</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi><mo>&minus;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">&Sigma;</mi><mrow><mo>&minus;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi><mo>&minus;</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(7.2)</span></div>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Transform standard-normal noise</h2>
<p>Equation (7.3) gives a practical recipe for generating correlated values. Start with a standard-normal vector <strong>z</strong>. The bold <em>I</em> is the <strong>identity matrix</strong>, meaning the starting coordinates have variance 1 and no covariance. Multiplying by matrix <strong>A</strong> scales and mixes the coordinates; adding &mu; translates the center. The condition <strong>AA</strong><sup>T</sup> = &Sigma; ensures that the generated vector has the desired covariance.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 7.3. Vector z is distributed as a multivariate normal with zero mean and identity covariance. Vector x equals mu plus A z, and A A transpose equals Sigma."><mtable columnalign="center" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">z</mi><mo>&sim;</mo><mi>𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">z</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace></mspace><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">&Sigma;</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(7.3)</span></div>
<p>Equation (7.4) shows a two-dimensional rotation matrix. The symbol &theta; is <strong>theta</strong> (pronounced &ldquo;THAY-ta&rdquo;) and represents the rotation angle. <strong>sin</strong> and <strong>cos</strong> are the sine and cosine functions. A rotation changes orientation without changing lengths. Rotating backward undoes the rotation, so the inverse matrix <strong>R</strong><sup>&minus;1</sup> is the same as the transpose <strong>R</strong><sup>T</sup>.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 7.4. R of theta is the two by two rotation matrix with cosine theta and sine theta entries. The inverse of R equals its transpose."><mtable columnalign="center" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>&theta;</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="true">(</mo><mtable columnalign="center center"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>&theta;</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>&minus;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>&theta;</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>&theta;</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mspace></mspace><mi>&theta;</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy="true">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mrow><mo>&minus;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(7.4)</span></div>
<p>An <strong>eigenvector</strong> identifies a direction that the covariance transformation stretches without turning away from that direction. Its <strong>eigenvalue</strong> tells how much variance lies along that direction, so the square root of the eigenvalue is the standard deviation there. The full derivation is optional. The required idea is that standard-normal noise can be scaled, rotated, and translated into a Gaussian with a chosen center, spread, and orientation.</p>
